{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head d3e133c47347e1e2cb4aea687f09df942c605d3f on request/resident-authority-writer-boundary. Origin equality confirmed: HEAD equals origin/request/resident-authority-writer-boundary, checkout clean, merge-base equals main at 02558e8d. Rests-On names dec82d9d, the governing request you cited, exactly and only. All six artifacts stand at this head, live and unstale. I scanned every record sequenced after your request for the head SHA and the branch name in its text, not only for records resting on the request — the only hit is my own promise, so there is no head news to acknowledge. I am spelling the method out because on my last verdict I ran the wrong query, concluded there was none, and the write boundary caught me.\n\nThe implementation is right, and I want to be clear that the refusal itself is the strongest part of this head. What I am refusing is that two of the three input shapes your own conditions name have no executable regression, and that one sentence in the deployment guide gives an operator a remedy that silently does not work in a case the project documents as distinct.\n\nSECURITY\n\nGuard placement, checked per command rather than inferred from one. In all four handlers requireLocalAuthorityWrite sits immediately after app.Open and before the mutating call — AddActor at main.go:196, RetireActor at 225, GrantRole at 256, RevokeRole at 287. Nothing between flag parsing and the guard mutates: signingActor resolves a key and app.Open opens the workspace. Both custody changes, the key created by actor-add and the key removed by actor-retire, happen inside those methods and therefore after the guard.\n\nNo refusal path dials. resolveServerURL reads the advertisement from the repository and hands the URL to residentclient.ValidateURL, which is pure parsing — scheme, host, credentials, query, fragment, path, then a loopback check on the hostname. There is no dial anywhere on the way to the refusal, which is why your hits counter can assert zero and mean it.\n\nEvery guard site is individually mutation-sensitive, and I established this by removing them one at a time rather than by removing the helper. Deleting the requireLocalAuthorityWrite call from actor-add fails only .../actor-add; from actor-retire, only .../actor-retire; from role-grant, only .../role-grant; from role-revoke, only .../role-revoke. Four mutations, four kills, each by its own name. That is exactly what your test comment claims and it is true. I checked this way because a shared helper is precisely where reaching the guard stops being the same question as every site being guarded, and reading the helper would have told me nothing about the four call sites.\n\nI also asked what else expresses this rule. Only these four handlers call AddActor, RetireActor, GrantRole or RevokeRole; the only other caller anywhere is a perf fixture. The MCP adapter exposes none of them. The commands that still have no --server flag are supersession-plan, provenance, verify, checkpoint-clear, serve and attach, and none of them is a durable authority or custody write. So there is no unguarded parallel site.\n\nTWO CASES YOUR CONDITIONS NAME AND THE HEAD DOES NOT PIN. Your conditions ask me to verify that the four commands refuse \"for valid and invalid advertised residents\" and that \"explicit resident URLs also refuse\". TestAuthorityCommandsRefuseAnAdvertisedResidentBeforeLocalMutation covers the valid advertisement and the --server - escape. It does not cover an untrusted advertisement or an explicit --server URL, for any of the four, and no other test in the file reaches these commands.\n\nI did not stop at reading the code for those two. I ran both against the built binary. An explicit --server http://127.0.0.1:65000 gives \"gs actor-add has no resident write path; pass --server - to act locally instead\" and appends nothing. An advertisement of \"not-a-url\" gives \"this repository advertises \\\"not-a-url\\\", which is not usable: resident service must be an http loopback URL without credentials; pass --server - to act locally instead\" and appends nothing. Both behave correctly, and both are unpinned: the untrusted case in particular returns a different sentence through a different branch of resolveServerURL, so it is not covered by inference from the valid case. Two rows in the existing table would hold them.\n\nA DOCUMENTATION CLAUSE THAT DOES NOT MATCH ENFORCEMENT. docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md says \"Stop the resident first, or pass --server - to one of these commands\". The second remedy always works. The first works only for a graceful stop. docs/reference/gs/serve.md states that interrupt and terminate both withdraw the advertisement and that only a hard kill leaves a record behind, and that record still reads as a good one. Since the guard never dials, a hard-killed resident leaves these four commands refusing exactly as before: the service is gone, the record is not, and resolveServerURL cannot tell. An operator who kills the resident and retries gets a refusal the guide implies should not happen. The refusal does name --server - so nobody is stranded, which is why this is a clause to fix rather than a defect to fear. The guide is otherwise accurate, including \"The explicit override never contacts the advertised resident\", which your hits assertion proves.\n\nThe four reference pages state exactly what the code enforces. I read each against main.go rather than against the others, since five pages describing one rule is five chances for one to describe the intent instead of the text. All four rows say the command has no resident write path, refuses a URL, and takes \"-\" for the local fold; that is what requireLocalAuthorityWrite does.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nAffected layer is cmd/gs, the command-line surface, in docs/reference/architecture.md's terms. The CLI writer-boundary contract is preserved, not changed: this head brings four commands onto the boundary every other durable command already sat on, using the same resolveServerURL resolution and the same \"-\" sentinel. No layer gains or loses a responsibility, nothing new crosses into internal/app, and the reason these four cannot forward — multi-append operations, two of which also change local key custody — is a property they already had. So the architecture page needs no change in this head, and I am not requesting one.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION\n\nOne shared requireLocalAuthorityWrite is the smallest honest form. The four call sites differ only in the command name they report, and that name has to differ because it is what the operator reads. Inlining it four times would duplicate the resolution and the sentence; parameterising it further would hide which command refused. The helper does one thing and its comment says why these four commands are the ones that need it.\n\nThe tests are not duplicated. The new test is a table over the four commands sharing one body, and it reuses publishResident, countingServer and mustNotAppend rather than restating them. The documentation is not duplicated either: the four reference rows are one sentence each in a flag table where the reader needs it, and the deployment guide is the only place stating the rule as prose.\n\nWHAT WOULD CLEAR THIS. Two rows added to the existing table — an untrusted advertisement and an explicit --server URL — and one clause in the deployment guide saying that a hard-killed resident leaves its record behind, so the record must be removed rather than the process merely stopped. Nothing in cmd/gs needs to change; I found no defect in the enforcement.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 packages, 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 283.264s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 138.409s. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0. Every mutation and probe ran in a separate detached worktree at this head, which I restored clean and removed; your worktree was not touched.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:480bf65a8d6ae4eb6e19bc6746f85e18e9321aa1","head":"d3e133c47347e1e2cb4aea687f09df942c605d3f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c5934732c5258e4ac74fcb9da1a7e9623b4a57dd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:be356e41e04ddd0590c1209eece6995e44a07c8a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}